



{"id":"3478ff43-7dc4-4ae4-aae7-359433e0f3ef","object":"chat.completion","created":1786449981,"model":"deepseek-v4-pro","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"record_id\":\"CA12_CORE_067\",\"decisions\":[{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_01\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_02\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_09\",\"depart_slot\":23,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_02\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_03\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_07\",\"depart_slot\":10,\"charge_blocks\":1},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_03\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_01\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_05\",\"depart_slot\":27,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_04\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_01\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_06\",\"depart_slot\":8,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_05\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_01\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_02\",\"depart_slot\":11,\"charge_blocks\":1},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_06\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_03\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_11\",\"depart_slot\":16,\"charge_blocks\":0}]}","reasoning_content":"We are given a public task for a UAV integrated-repair optimization task. We must return one JSON object with the decisions. The scenario is a \"CANDIDATE A v1.2.1\" repair. The instance includes a baseline solution, but we need to produce a repair for the perturbed scenario. The assignment order contract is \"FIXED_UAV_ASSIGNMENT_AND_FIXED_UAV_ORDER\", meaning each mission is already assigned to a specific UAV and must be executed in a fixed order per UAV. The deadhead contract is \"FIXED_REGISTERED_INCOMING_DEADHEAD_PATH\", but incoming deadhead is null for all missions. So we must schedule these missions.\n\nLet's list missions with their fixed UAV and sequence index.\n\n- MISSION_01: uav_id: UAV_02, sequence_index: 0, release 23, due 30, origin VP_03, dest VP_07, payload 0.5 kg. Options: PATH_04 (compatible false), PATH_09 (compatible true). Only compatible path: PATH_09. Travel slots 2, energy 0.12543.\n- MISSION_02: uav_id: UAV_03, sequence_index: 0, release 7, due 13, origin VP_02, dest VP_03, payload 0.5 kg. Options: PATH_03 (compatible true), PATH_07 (compatible true), PATH_10 (compatible true). Travel slots: 3, 2, 7 respectively. Energy: 0.263403, 0.25086, 4.39005 respectively.\n- MISSION_03: uav_id: UAV_01, sequence_index: 2, release 26, due 30, origin VP_01, dest VP_04, payload 0.5 kg. Only option PATH_05, travel slots 1, energy 1.208.\n- MISSION_04: uav_id: UAV_01, sequence_index: 0, release 7, due 9, origin VP_02, dest VP_03, payload 0.5 kg. Only option PATH_06, travel slots 2, energy 0.16308.\n- MISSION_05: uav_id: UAV_01, sequence_index: 1, release 10, due 27, origin VP_03, dest VP_01, payload 0.5 kg. Options: PATH_01 (travel 2, energy 0.151), PATH_02 (travel 14, energy 1.28652), PATH_08 (travel 2, energy 0.151). All compatible? Check: PATH_01 uses COR_11? Actually PATH_01: corridor_ids: [\"COR_11\"], travel 2, energy 0.151. PATH_02: COR_08, travel 14. PATH_08: COR_03, travel 2. All appear compatible. But note: interventions include closed corridors: COR_03 closed slots 10-49 (i.e., from slot 10 to 49), COR_11 closed slots 10-21. So PATH_08 uses COR_03, which is closed from slot 10 onward. Since MISSION_05 release is 10, if we depart at or after 10, we cannot use PATH_08 because the corridor is closed. PATH_01 uses COR_11, closed from slot 10 to 21. So if we depart in that slot range, cannot use PATH_01 either. PATH_02 uses COR_08, which is not closed. So we may need to choose PATH_02 if necessary. But also consider energy and travel time. We'll need to check timeline for UAV_01: missions sequence: index 0: M04, index 1: M05, index 2: M03. So UAV_01 must do M04 first, then M05, then M03, in that order, all starting from its start vertiport? Actually UAV_01 starts at VP_02 at slot 7 (available_slot 7). M04 origin VP_02, so it can start there. After M04, it ends at VP_03. Then M05 origin VP_03, so can depart from there. After M05, ends at VP_01. Then M03 origin VP_01, so can depart from there. So good. Need to compute earliest possible start times and check due dates, reserve energy, charging.\n\n- MISSION_06: uav_id: UAV_03, sequence_index: 1, release 16, due 25, origin VP_03, dest VP_05, payload 0.5 kg. Only option PATH_11, travel slots 1, energy 0.100344.\n\nAlso note: UAV_03 has two missions: M02 (seq 0) and M06 (seq 1). It starts at VP_02 (start_vertiport for UAV_03 is VP_02? Actually fleet: UAV_03 start_vertiport: \"VP_02\". Its initial_soc_kwh is overridden: initial_soc_overrides: 1.807303. The original initial_soc_kwh was 6.990394, but override gives 1.807303. So UAV_03 starts with only 1.807303 kWh. It has battery capacity 16.0 kWh, reserve 1.5 kWh. Its empty_energy_kwh_per_km: 0.1, payload_energy_kwh_per_km_kg: 0.026. Travel time coefficient: 1.0. Max wind level 1. Compatible layers: [\"L120_220\",\"L220_320\"]. All corridors are L120_220, so compatible.\n\nUAV_02: start VP_03, available slot 7, initial_soc_kwh 7.5, battery capacity 16, reserve 1.5, empty energy 0.1, payload energy 0.026, travel coeff 1.0, max wind 1. It has only M01: release 23, due 30, origin VP_03, dest VP_07. It starts at VP_03, so can do it.\n\nUAV_01: start VP_02, available slot 7, initial_soc_kwh 4.6826, battery 22, reserve 2.5, empty energy 0.145, payload energy 0.012, travel coeff 0.86, max wind 2. Compatible layers: [\"L120_220\",\"L220_320\"]. Missions: M04, M05, M03. Need to compute energy for each path with payload 0.5 kg. Energy per km: empty + payload * payload_energy. But given the path options, they provide energy_kwh directly, which likely already accounts for payload? The task says energy_kwh is the energy required for the flight. So we can use that. Then we need to add incoming deadhead? None. Then after service flight, we do reserve check, destination turnaround (1 slot), and post-service charge blocks. For each UAV, we must ensure at the start of each mission, available energy >= incoming deadhead (if any) + service flight energy + reserve. After the service flight, we can charge. SOC after charging cannot exceed battery capacity. Charger capacity applies: each vertiport has charger_count 2, so no more than 2 simultaneous charging at a port, unless overridden (none). And pad capacity: each vertiport 2 pads, except VP_03 at slot 9 has pad capacity override to 1 (only at slot 9). That means only one UAV can be at VP_03 at slot 9 (arriving or departing? Pad capacity is for the vertiport itself, generally for occupancy. The pad capacity by slot default is 2. Override: slot 9, VP_03 pad_capacity 1. So at slot 9, only one UAV can be at VP_03. This could affect simultaneous arrivals/departures or turnaround occupancy. We need to ensure that at any slot, the number of UAVs at a vertiport (including after arrival and before departure, i.e., during turnaround/charging) does not exceed pad capacity. Also we need to consider corridor capacity: each corridor has capacity 2 per slot, but not mentioned overrides. The interventions: closed slots for COR_03 from 10-49, COR_11 from 10-21. So these corridors are fully closed in those slots. Also there is an initial_soc_override for UAV_03 to 1.807303. That is important.\n\nNow we need to produce decisions for each mission: mission_id, uav_id, held (true/false), path_id, depart_slot, charge_blocks. All missions must be served if possible. Held can be true if we cannot meet constraints? The task says \"Respect fixed UAV assignment and mission order, route compatibility, release/due windows, incoming deadhead, charging, SOC/reserve, corridor/pad/charger capacity, closures, and all intervention overrides. Optimize the stated task lexicographically: urgent holds, total holds, repair distance, weighted tardiness, delay plus charge wait, then energy.\" Since we are to produce a repair, we should try to schedule all without holding unless necessary. The baseline had no holds. But with perturbations, maybe we need to hold some? The interventions could force delays, but we might still manage within windows by choosing paths and charging.\n\nWe must output exactly one JSON of the form: {\"record_id\":\"CA12_CORE_067\",\"decisions\":[...]} with each mission.\n\nLet's analyze each UAV.\n\nFirst, UAV_01:\n- Start: VP_02, available slot 7, initial SOC 4.6826 kWh.\n- Missions in order: M04 (release 7, due 9), M05 (release 10, due 27), M03 (release 26, due 30).\n\nM04: origin VP_02 -> VP_03, option PATH_06 (travel 2 slots, energy 0.16308). If depart at slot 7, arrival at slot 7+2-1? Need to understand slot semantics. \"depart_slot\" is the start slot of travel. The travel lasts travel_slots slots. Usually, if depart_slot = s, then the service flight occupies slots [s, s+travel_slots-1] (assuming discrete slots). Then after arrival, turnaround is 1 slot. In the normative order: incoming deadhead -> service flight -> reserve check -> destination turnaround -> post-service charge. So after service flight, we do reserve check immediately. Then we do turnaround (duration 1 slot). Then post-service charge blocks. So if we depart slot s, travel slots = t, arrival is at slot s+t-1? Actually, if service flight starts at slot s and lasts t slots, then it finishes at the end of slot s+t-1. Turnaround occupies the next slot (s+t). Then charge blocks can start at slot s+t+1? Possibly. But need to check exact definition: \"destination turnaround\" occurs after service flight and before post-service charge. Typically, turnaround is an immediate slot after arrival. So if arrival is at the end of slot s+t-1, then the UAV is available at the vertiport from slot s+t for turnaround. The turnaround takes 1 slot, so the UAV is occupied until the end of that slot. Then charging can start in the next slot. But note: the UAV might also be idle after turnaround before the next mission. Also, the post-service charge is the only charging opportunity, and it's at the destination. The charge_blocks attached to a mission occur at that mission's destination after turnaround. The number of charge_blocks is an integer 0..max_charge_blocks (3). Each block adds charge_kwh_per_block to SOC, but cannot exceed battery capacity. The charging takes place over consecutive slots starting after turnaround. The charging uses a charger, so we must ensure charger capacity at that vertiport is not exceeded during those charge slots. Also, the pad capacity must accommodate the UAV during charging and turnaround. The UAV is at the vertiport from the arrival slot until departure for the next mission (or end). So pad occupancy: from arrival slot through turnaround, charging, and idle until departure. For each slot, the UAV counts against pad capacity. At VP_03, pad capacity override at slot 9 reduces capacity to 1. So any UAV at VP_03 at slot 9 must be alone.\n\nLet's compute earliest possible for M04. release 7. So depart_slot >= 7. Travel 2 slots, so arrival slot = depart_slot+1? Let's think: if depart_slot = 7, then flight is during slots 7 and 8? Travel_slots=2, so it occupies slots 7 and 8, arrival at the end of slot 8? Then turnaround is slot 9? But pad capacity at slot 9 at VP_03 is 1, so we can only have at most one UAV at VP_03 at slot 9. UAV_01 doing M04 arrives at VP_03 at slot 8 end, so it needs pad occupancy at slot 8 (arrival) and slot 9 (turnaround). At slot 9, it would be occupying one pad. If another UAV (like UAV_03 or UAV_02) also is at VP_03 at slot 9, that could violate capacity 1. So we need to check other UAVs. Let's see other missions: UAV_02 starts at VP_03 at slot 7. So at slot 7, UAV_02 is at VP_03. It might depart after slot 7. Since its mission M01 release slot 23, it might remain idle at VP_03 until then. If UAV_02 stays at VP_03 from slot 7 to slot 23, it occupies a pad at VP_03. That would mean at slot 9, it's there. Then UAV_01 arriving at VP_03 at slot 8 end and doing turnaround at slot 9 would conflict because pad capacity at VP_03 slot 9 is 1. Thus UAV_01 cannot be at VP_03 at slot 9 if UAV_02 is already there. UAV_02 available slot 7 at VP_03, but it could also depart earlier if it had a mission, but its only mission has release 23. It could idle there. Unless we can force UAV_02 to leave VP_03? But it can only leave by doing a mission. It could deadhead out? But no deadhead except for missions. So UAV_02 will be at VP_03 from slot 7 until it departs for M01 at depart_slot >= 23. So it will occupy VP_03 continuously. So UAV_01's M04, which goes to VP_03, would conflict with UAV_02 if it arrives before UAV_02 departs. M04 due 9, so we need to complete it by due 9? Due slot is the latest arrival? Usually due slot is when the mission should be completed, meaning the service flight must finish by due slot? The task says: \"due_slot\": 9, so the arrival should be no later than slot 9? Actually \"due_slot\" is the deadline for the mission, probably meaning the service flight must be completed (i.e., arrival at destination) by that slot. So M04 due 9 means arrival at VP_03 must be at or before slot 9. So if we depart at slot 7, travel 2 slots, arrival at end of slot 8, that's before slot 9, okay. If we depart at 8, arrival end of slot 9, that is also okay because arrival at slot 9 end? But due slot 9, so maybe arrival slot must be <= 9, so depart 8 arrival at 9 is exactly on due. But then turnaround at slot 10, which is after due. But due only applies to the service flight completion? So depart 8 might work. But there is pad conflict with UAV_02 if UAV_02 is still at VP_03 at slot 8/9/10. UAV_02 stays till slot 23, so it's at VP_03 the whole time. So any arrival of UAV_01 at VP_03 before UAV_02 departs would cause at least 2 UAVs at VP_03, but pad capacity is 2 default, except slot 9 when it is 1. So if UAV_01 arrives at VP_03 at slot 8, then during slot 8, there would be UAV_02 and UAV_01 (2 pads) okay. At slot 9, UAV_01 is still there for turnaround, and UAV_02 is there, that's 2 but capacity is 1 -> conflict. So to avoid conflict, UAV_01 must depart at a time such that its turnaround slot is not 9. Turnaround slot = depart_slot + travel_slots (since turnaround is the slot after arrival? Actually: if depart=s, travel=t, service flight occupies slots s..s+t-1, arrival at end of slot s+t-1. Turnaround occupies the very next slot (s+t). So turnaround slot = s+t. For travel_slots=2, turnaround slot = s+2. To avoid slot 9, s+2 != 9, i.e., s != 7. So if s=7, turnaround slot=9 (conflict). If s=8, travel=2, turnaround slot=10 (okay, pad cap at VP_03 at slot 10 is 2, so two UAVs fine). But release is 7, so s can be 7 or later. s=8 possible. But M04 due 9, s=8 arrival end of slot 9, left? Arrival slot = s+1 (since travel 2 slots: depart in slot 8, flight occupies slots 8 and 9? Actually if depart_slot=8, does the flight begin at the start of slot 8? It likely occupies the slot. So service flight would be slots 8 and 9, arrival at the end of slot 9. That is due slot 9, so it's on time. Then turnaround slot 10. So that is feasible. So we could schedule M04 depart slot 8 to avoid pad capacity violation at slot 9. Then after turnaround, we can charge if needed, and then depart for M05.\n\nBut also need to check initial energy. UAV_01 start SOC 4.6826, M04 energy 0.16308, reserve 2.5. So at departure for M04, we need SOC >= 0.16308 + 2.5 = 2.66308. SOC is 4.6826, ok. After flight, SOC drops by 0.16308 to 4.51952. Reserve check immediately after service flight: check that SOC after service flight (but before reserve check?) The order says: incoming deadhead -> service flight -> reserve check. So after service flight, we check if SOC >= reserve. Actually reserve check is to ensure that after the service flight, the UAV still has at least reserve energy. But SOC after service flight is 4.51952, which is >2.5, ok. Then turnaround, then charge if any. We can decide charge_blocks for M04. If we charge, we could increase SOC. Then for M05, flight from VP_03 to VP_01. We need to choose path and ensure energy. M05 release 10, due 27. So after M04 turnaround at slot 10, we could start M05 as early as slot 10? But M05 release is 10, so depart_slot >= 10. So if M04 turnaround ends at slot 10, we can depart for M05 at slot 10? But M04 turnaround occupies slot 10? Turnaround is 1 slot, so if turnaround slot is 10, then the UAV is busy during slot 10? The turnaround slot is the destination turnaround, which typically is a mandatory wait after arrival before the UAV can be used for the next mission. The normative order: after service flight and reserve check, we do destination turnaround, then post-service charge. So if service flight ends at end of slot 9, touchdown, then reserve check happens (instant?), then turnaround begins at start of slot 10? The turnaround likely takes the full slot 10. So the UAV is not available to start a new mission until after turnaround is complete, i.e., at the start of slot 11. So depart_slot for next mission must be >= 11. However, if we do charging after turnaround, that adds more slots. So if we do no charging, depart for M05 could be slot 11? But M05 release is 10, so slot 11 is >= 10, okay. But we must also consider that UAV_01 after M04 is at VP_03, and for M05 origin is VP_03, so that's fine. So earliest depart for M05 would be slot 11 if no charge. However, M05 travel time depends on path chosen. PATH_01 or PATH_08 travel 2 slots, arrival slot = depart+1. Due 27, so fine. PATH_02 travel 14 slots, arrival slot = depart+13. That could be later but within due if depart early enough.\n\nNow, M05: paths: PATH_01 (COR_11) travel 2, energy 0.151; PATH_08 (COR_03) travel 2, energy 0.151; PATH_02 (COR_08) travel 14, energy 1.28652. But interventions: COR_11 closed slots 10-21; COR_03 closed slots 10-49. So if depart_slot is within [10,21], PATH_01 cannot be used because COR_11 is closed in those slots. If depart_slot is within [10,49], PATH_08 cannot be used because COR_03 closed. So the only feasible path for many depart slots is PATH_02, which uses COR_08 (not closed). COR_08 base travel time 71.35 mins? Actually from topology, COR_08 from VP_03 to VP_01 has base_travel_time_min 71.35, distance 8.0 km. But travel_slots for PATH_02 is 14 slots. That's a long travel. But due 27, so if we depart early enough, we could still make it. For UAV_01, we need to check energy for PATH_02: 1.28652 kWh. After M04 with possible charging, we need enough energy. Let's compute SOC after M04 turnaround without charging: 4.51952. For M05, reserve 2.5, service flight energy 1.28652 => required SOC at start of M05: 1.28652 + 2.5 = 3.78652. SOC 4.51952 is enough, so we could do M05 PATH_02 without charging. But if we choose PATH_01 or PATH_08 energy 0.151, required 0.151+2.5=2.651, still fine. But those paths are closed if depart_slot is in certain ranges. Since M05 release 10, early depart slots like 11, 12, etc., COR_11 closed 10-21, so for depart slot 11, it's closed, so PATH_01 not allowed. For depart slot 22, COR_11 reopens? Closed slots 10-21 inclusive, so from slot 22 it's open. But then PATH_08 is closed until slot 49, so not allowed. So between release 10 and due 27, only PATH_02 might be feasible, unless we can delay departure until after slot 21 to use PATH_01. Slot 22 is after COR_11 closure, so PATH_01 could be used. PATH_08 closed till 49, so never. So we have two options for M05: depart between [10,21] using PATH_02 (travel 14), or depart at [22,?] using PATH_01 (travel 2). To minimize delay, we might want to depart at slot 11 with PATH_02, but that would arrive at VP_01 at slot 11+13=24? Actually travel_slots=14, so if depart 11, service flight occupies slots 11..24, arrival at end of slot 24. Turnaround slot 25. Then M03 release 26, origin VP_01, so can depart at 26. That would work, due 30, so fine. Alternatively, wait to depart M05 at slot 22 with PATH_01: arrival at end of slot 23, turnaround slot 24, then M03 depart at slot 25? But M03 release 26, so must wait until 26, so depart M03 at 26 anyway. Further, waiting longer might cause tardiness? All due dates are met.\n\nBut also we need to consider UAV_02 at VP_03 after M04. Actually UAV_02 start VP_03, it will be there from slot 7 until it departs for M01. M01 release 23, due 30. UAV_02 could depart at 23 earliest. So during slots 7-22, UAV_02 is at VP_03. UAV_01 after M04 turnaround at slot 10, and if we do charging or idle, UAV_01 will also be at VP_03 until it departs for M05. At VP_03, pad capacity is 2 except slot 9 cap 1. At slot 10 onwards, cap 2. So two UAVs allowed. So UAV_01 and UAV_02 can both be at VP_03 simultaneously. No conflict.\n\nNow for M03: UAV_01 after M05 arrives at VP_01, M03 origin VP_01, release 26, due 30. So schedule M03 depart_slot >= 26. Travel 1 slot (PATH_05), energy 1.208. Reserve 2.5. So required SOC at start of M03: 1.208 + 2.5 = 3.708. We must have enough SOC after M05 and any charging at VP_01. If we chose M05 PATH_02, after M05 arrival SOC = previous SOC - 1.28652, then reserve check, turnaround, charge. Let's compute. Starting SOC after M04 turnaround 4.51952, then we may or may not charge for M04. If we don't charge, then for M05, departure SOC 4.51952, after flight SOC = 4.51952 - 1.28652 = 3.233. After reserve check (ok, 3.233 > 2.5), turnaround at VP_01. Then we can charge for M05 (charge_blocks). If we do 0 charge, SOC 3.233 for M03, but M03 requires 3.708, so not enough. So we need to charge after M05 at VP_01 to increase SOC to at least 3.708. Battery capacity 22, charge_kwh_per_block for UAV_01 is 0.95. So we need at least 3.708 - 3.233 = 0.475 kWh. One block gives 0.95, so one charge block would bring SOC to 3.233+0.95=4.183, which is fine, and below cap 22. So we could schedule 1 charge block for M05 at VP_01. Then after charging, SOC 4.183. Then M03 depart. Note: M03 turnaround? Actually after M05, we do turnaround at VP_01 (1 slot), then charge blocks, then idle until M03 depart. M03 release 26, so we need to ensure that after turnaround and charging, we are ready by 26. If M05 arrives at end of slot 24, turnaround slot 25, charge could start at slot 26? Need to allocate charge slots. Each charge block takes 1 slot? The description says \"post-service charge\" with charge_blocks indicating number of blocks. Each block charges a fixed amount. Typically, charging takes one slot per block, but not explicitly stated. Usually, charge rate is per block, and each block consumes a slot. The task says \"charge_kwh_per_block\": 0.95, and \"max_charge_blocks\": 3. So likely, each charge block adds energy and occupies one slot. So if we set charge_blocks = 1 for M05, then after turnaround, the UAV will occupy a charger for 1 slot. At VP_01, charger capacity 2. So we need to ensure no conflict. After turnaround at slot 25, charging would happen during slot... when? The normative order says: \"destination turnaround -> post-service charge\". So after turnaround, the UAV can start charging. Typically, turnaround is a slot where the UAV cannot do anything else, then charging begins in the next slot and lasts for charge_blocks slots. So if turnaround is slot 25, then charging could occur in slot 26? But M03 depart_slot might be 26 or later. If charge block occupies slot 26, then UAV cannot depart for M03 at slot 26 because it's charging? Possibly, the charge occurs before the next mission's deadhead/flight. The SOC after charging is available to later missions. But the UAV may not be able to depart while charging. So if we need to depart M03 at slot 26, we cannot be charging at slot 26. So we should schedule charging to finish before depart. For M05, we could either do no charging, but then insufficient SOC. So maybe we need to charge after M04 instead, or after M05 but delay M03 departure. Let's see alternatives: Could we charge after M04 at VP_03, so that before M05 we have more SOC? M04 turnaround at slot 10 (if depart 8) or slot 9 (if depart 7) but slot 9 conflict. So if depart 8, turnaround slot 10, then we could charge at VP_03. Charging would increase SOC, perhaps allowing M05 PATH_02 without needing to char